    "math_academy_id": "q-79301",
    "knowledge_point_id": "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8",
    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096769/assets/b3bb8c00ab2e0ef3e7c1bc21726458e718e67897f28093321884bd1c67ca768f.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?",
    "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-4,-1)$ and passes through the point $(5,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-4,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}=4p(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(5,5),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5+1)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4(0+4) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ y & =-5,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-5$ and $3.$",
